(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-general-dermatology-i-vignettes"]={"title":"General Dermatology I Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has chronic bilateral lower-leg swelling, itching and skin discoloration. Examination shows poorly demarcated erythematous plaques, pitting oedema and brown hyperpigmentation around both medial ankles. Pedal pulses are diminished. Which is the most appropriate next step before prescribing compression therapy?","choices":["Begin oral cephalexin","Perform an ankle-brachial index","Obtain a bacterial wound culture","Order patch testing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Compression is the cornerstone of management, but only once adequate arterial circulation is established, and the diminished pulses demand that check first. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with cutaneous lichen planus on the wrists has not improved with emollients. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Superpotent topical steroids","Topical tacrolimus ointment","Oral prednisone","PUVA phototherapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Topical tacrolimus is used for oral and vaginal disease, oral steroids for severe cases, and phototherapy for refractory disease. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 178"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has a mildly inflamed eczematous patch on the eyelid and another on the vulva. Which topical corticosteroid is most appropriate?","choices":["Clobetasol propionate 0.05%, for a short course","Betamethasone dipropionate 0.05% twice daily","Triamcinolone acetonide 0.1% twice daily","Hydrocortisone, or another low potency agent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — potency is chosen by site, and these are the thinnest skin in the body. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has plaques on both elbows that have been present for two years. The clinical picture is not clear-cut and treatment has failed. Which test would settle the diagnosis?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide preparation","Serum HLA-B27","Direct immunofluorescence","Skin biopsy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — psoriasis is generally a clinical diagnosis, though biopsy may be needed for definitive diagnosis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 162"},{"q":"An 80-year-old woman has bullous pemphigoid limited to a few areas of the trunk. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Oral prednisone with azathioprine","Rituximab","Low-dose methotrexate with folic acid","Ultrapotent topical steroids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — mild disease is managed topically, with oral prednisone or doxycycline reserved for moderate to severe disease. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"A 29-year-old hairdresser has a three-week history of intensely itchy small blisters along the sides of her fingers and on her palms. Examination shows grouped 1 to 2 mm vesicles described as resembling tapioca, on a poorly defined erythematous base, with surrounding dryness. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nummular eczema","Dyshidrotic eczema","Allergic contact dermatitis","Bullous pemphigoid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tapioca vesicles on the palms and lateral fingers are the defining picture; vesicles may coalesce into bullae before desquamating. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman develops an itchy eruption under a new necklace and beneath the button of her jeans. Which allergen is most likely?","choices":["Nickel","Urushiol","Neomycin","Bacitracin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — nickel is the most common metal allergen, and eruptions reproduce the shape of the item worn. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has a single intensely itchy, well-demarcated thickened plaque on her lateral ankle with exaggerated skin markings and pigment change. She admits to scratching it most evenings while watching television. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Break the itch-scratch cycle through education, emollients, behavioural substitution and nail care, with a limited course of a potent topical steroid","Excise the plaque","Begin oral antibiotics","Begin an oral antifungal such as terbinafine, on the basis that the thickened scaly plaque is most likely a fungal infection of long standing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — recurrence is common unless the initiating itch is controlled. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 183"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with perioral dermatitis has stopped her topical corticosteroid. Which is the most appropriate counselling point about what happens next?","choices":["The condition will not recur once the corticosteroid has been stopped once","The eruption may temporarily worsen after corticosteroid withdrawal before it improves","The eruption should clear completely within 48 hours of stopping","She should resume the steroid if the rash worsens at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — telling her in advance is what stops her restarting the steroid. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a six-week history of painful mouth erosions and now has flaccid blisters on the scalp and chest that rupture easily and bleed. Lateral pressure on adjacent normal skin causes the top layer to slough. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bullous pemphigoid","Erythema multiforme","Lichen planus","Pemphigus vulgaris"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oral onset, flaccid easily ruptured bullae and a positive Nikolsky sign are the triad, reflecting intraepithelial acantholysis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 130"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man with pityriasis rosea asks what to expect. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["It requires a six-week course of oral antibiotics","It will persist indefinitely without phototherapy","It is highly contagious by direct skin contact, so he should avoid close contact with others until every last lesion has faded","It is self-limited and typically fades over 4 to 6 weeks, though darker skin may show post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation for several months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — reassurance is the mainstay, with antihistamines or topical steroids used cautiously for itch. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 171"},{"q":"A mother of a child newly diagnosed with atopic dermatitis is anxious about using topical steroids at all. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Advise avoiding all topical steroids and relying on emollients alone","Advise applying the steroid generously over the whole body twice daily","Advise that the condition resolves permanently by school age, so consistent daily treatment is optional and can be stopped whenever the skin looks clear","Address the concern directly, review the side effects, demonstrate how much to apply, and provide a written flare and infection action plan"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Addressing steroid concerns directly and demonstrating quantity and application are named education points. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has well-demarcated salmon-pink plaques with thick silvery scale over both elbows, knees and the sacrum. Scraping the scale produces pinpoint bleeding. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Lichen simplex chronicus","Plaque psoriasis","Nummular eczema","Seborrhoeic dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The extensor distribution with silvery scale and the Auspitz sign (bleeding on scale removal) are classic. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 159"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman noticed a single 3 cm oval pink patch on her back ten days ago; the centre has since cleared leaving a fine rim of scale. Now she has many smaller similar oval lesions across her trunk, their long axes running along the skin cleavage lines. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea corporis","Nummular eczema","Pityriasis rosea","Guttate psoriasis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The herald patch with a collarette of scale, followed 1 to 2 weeks later by lesions in a Christmas tree pattern, is the picture. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 169"},{"q":"A 44-year-old janitor has chronic dry, fissured, glazed dermatitis of both hands from repeated detergent exposure. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Refer for patch testing before any treatment","Avoid the exposure and repair the barrier with emollients, sleeping in cotton gloves after applying a heavy emollient such as petroleum jelly","Begin a high-potency topical corticosteroid indefinitely, continuing it daily even once the hands have healed, without addressing the exposure","Begin oral doxycycline"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with antihistamines such as hydroxyzine or diphenhydramine for itch. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 3-month-old infant has thick, greasy yellow scale adherent to the scalp, with mild erythema and no distress. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Atopic dermatitis","Tinea capitis","Psoriasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the scalp form is dandruff in adults and this presentation in infants, driven by Malassezia in sebum-rich areas. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 9-month-old has a bright red rash in the diaper area. Examination shows beefy erythema involving the inguinal folds with peripheral scale and scattered satellite papules and pustules beyond the main area. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Candidal diaper dermatitis","Irritant diaper dermatitis","Bacterial diaper dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fold involvement with satellite lesions is the candidal pattern; irritant disease spares the folds. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with darker skin had a single large oval patch on the trunk two weeks ago, followed by smaller oval lesions along the skin lines. The eruption is now fading but leaving brown marks. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The brown marks mean the eruption was misdiagnosed and a biopsy is now needed","The brown marks indicate secondary infection and need a topical antibiotic","Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation is common here and can last several months, but it does resolve","The brown marks are permanent and will require long-term treatment with a depigmenting agent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the eruption itself is self-limiting and leaves no scar. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 169"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with deeply pigmented skin has months of itching and swelling of both lower legs. The gaiter regions show poorly demarcated violaceous-to-deep-brown patches, and on palpation both areas are warm and boggy. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nummular eczema of both lower extremities","Stasis dermatitis","Bilateral cellulitis of the lower legs","Allergic contact dermatitis to a topical product"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — on darker skin the erythema reads as violaceous, grey or brown, so palpation for warmth and oedema carries the diagnosis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 114"},{"q":"A 5-year-old with atopic dermatitis has an area of weeping, thickly crusted plaques with pustules over a previously flaring elbow. She is afebrile. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic test?","choices":["Patch testing","Potassium hydroxide preparation","Bacterial culture of the crusted lesions","Herpes simplex virus polymerase chain reaction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — culture is indicated for purulent, pustular or significantly crusted lesions to rule out infection. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has painful oral erosions and flaccid skin bullae. A biopsy has been taken. Which finding on that biopsy would establish the diagnosis?","choices":["Band-like lymphocytic infiltration of the dermis","Hyperkeratosis without parakeratosis and Civatte bodies","Acantholysis","Neutrophils aligned in a straight narrow row at the dermal-epidermal junction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — loss of keratinocyte-to-keratinocyte adhesion is the defining histological feature, with immunofluorescence and serum ELISA confirmatory. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 132"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has coin-shaped scaly plaques on both shins. A potassium hydroxide preparation is negative and there is no purulence. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Medium to high potency topical corticosteroids with emollients","Oral terbinafine","Compression therapy","Ketoconazole shampoo applied to all the affected areas"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — steroids for the active lesions and emollients to restore the barrier and prevent recurrence. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man wishes to treat male-pattern hair loss and asks about finasteride. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The sexual side effects are permanent once they occur","It is most effective at the frontal hairline","It can safely be shared with a female partner who is also experiencing hair thinning at the crown","About 2% of men report reduced libido and erectile function, and these effects reverse when the drug is stopped"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It works best combined with minoxidil, and it is not indicated in women and contraindicated in pregnancy. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman notices two smooth, round 3 cm bald patches on her scalp that appeared over a few weeks. The scalp is smooth without scale or erythema, and short hairs tapering toward the root are visible at the margins. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Alopecia areata","Tinea capitis","Trichotillomania","Androgenetic alopecia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Exclamation point hairs (thinner at the root, normal calibre at the top) are pathognomonic, and the scalp is smooth without scale. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 138"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has a soft subcutaneous nodule on the forearm and the clinician wants to know whether it is fluid-filled. Which bedside technique addresses that?","choices":["Diascopy with a Wood's lamp","Mineral oil preparation","Transillumination","Dermoscopy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a fluid-filled lesion glows when a light is placed against it. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"An 8-month-old has erythema, scaling and small erosions over the convex surfaces of the buttocks and upper thighs, with the inguinal folds clearly spared. There is no purulence. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["A high-potency topical corticosteroid applied at every change until the rash has completely cleared","Frequent diaper changes, gentle cleansing, air exposure and a thick zinc oxide or petrolatum barrier at each change","A topical antifungal at every change","Oral antibiotics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — reducing moisture and irritant exposure is the foundation, and the fold sparing indicates irritant rather than candidal disease. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has recurrent scaling of the scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds and external ears. Examination shows greasy, yellow-white scale over mildly erythematous poorly demarcated patches. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Ketoconazole shampoo and topical ketoconazole cream","Oral terbinafine","High-potency topical corticosteroids as monotherapy","Topical mupirocin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Topical antifungals are the mainstay because Malassezia overgrowth drives the condition; steroids may be added early to reduce inflammation. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 30-year-old has extensive urushiol contact dermatitis covering both arms, the neck and part of the trunk. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["A five-day course of oral prednisone without any taper","Oral antihistamines alone","High-dose oral corticosteroids tapered over two to three weeks","High-potency topical corticosteroids alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — extensive disease needs systemic therapy, and early discontinuation causes high rates of rebound. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy with darker skin has a chronic itchy eruption in both antecubital and popliteal fossae. The plaques look silvery rather than red, with excoriation and thickened skin markings. His mother has asthma. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea corporis of both flexures","Lichen simplex chronicus at both sites","Atopic dermatitis","Plaque psoriasis of the flexures"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — flexural distribution with a family history of atopy; on darker skin the plaques can appear almost silvery instead of inflamed. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man reports generalised itching and rough, tight skin each winter, worst on the shins. Examination shows fine scale with a few shallow fissures and scratch marks, without erythematous plaques. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Take short lukewarm showers with a gentle fragrance-free cleanser only where needed, and apply a thick ointment or cream within minutes of bathing","Take long hot showers each morning to hydrate the skin thoroughly, then apply a light lotion once the skin has completely dried","Use an antibacterial soap over the whole body twice daily","Apply a keratolytic containing urea to the fissured areas first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — hot water and detergents are among the causes, and prompt moisturising after bathing is the key manoeuvre. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 153"}]};
